    82	   Number::toString, the serialization RFC 8785 §3.2.2.3 uses.
    83	
    84	Accepted: `9.8`, `9.80`, `1E2`, `1e21`, `5e-324`, `123456789012345680000` and `0e99999999999999999999`. Each keeps its
    85	value, and only the spelling changes.
    86	
    87	Rejected, each with its reason:
    88	- `1e400`: overflow.
    89	- `1e-400`: nonzero, and rounds to 0.
    90	- `333333333.33333329` and `9007199254740993`: the value would change.
    91	- `0.1000000000000000055511151231257827021181583404541015625`: this is *exactly* a double, but RFC 8785 prints it as
    92	  `0.1`, which has a different value.
    93	- `4.9e-324`: rounds to the smallest subnormal, which prints as `5e-324`.
    94	- `-0`.
    95	- `NaN` and `Infinity`: not JSON at all.
    96	
    97	Subnormals that survive rule 3 are accepted, so G-03b is reversed: RFC 8785 treats them as ordinary.
    98	
    99	**Why this rule and not the registered literal rule.** Read literally, "exactly representable as a double" does
   100	not compose with RFC 8785. It *accepts* `0.1000000000000000055511151231257827021181583404541015625`, whose
   101	canonical form `0.1` it then *rejects* when the verifier re-reads the payload. So under that reading,
   102	`canonical()` maps its own domain outside itself. The value-preserving rule is the largest rule under which:
   103	- (a) canonicalization never changes a value; and
   104	- (b) every canonical output is itself accepted.
   105	**This is a change to A1 P8's rejection item and prereg §4.3's wording, and it needs an author sentence (§9, D-1).**
   106	The V1 derivation's profile line, "registered number rule unchanged", must be reconciled with it either way.
   107	
   108	What "never helpfully normalized" means here. The **issuer's ingestion** normalizes *spelling only*:
   109	- insignificant whitespace;
   110	- member order;
   111	- escape choice;
   112	- number spelling.
   113	
   114	It never changes a value. The **verifier normalizes nothing**: a payload must already equal `JCS` of its own
   115	parse (§1.4, `JSON_NONCANONICAL`). Injectivity (PROOF.md) is stated over *values*. Two texts that differ only in
   116	spelling are one value by definition, and they get one canonical byte string. That is intended.
   117	
   118	### 1.3 Serialization
   119	
   120	`canonical(v) = JCS(v)` (RFC 8785):
   121	- UTF-8 output, with no insignificant whitespace;
   122	- object members sorted by the UTF-16 code units of their names;
   123	- numbers printed by `ES`;
   124	- strings escaped only for `"`, `\` and U+0000 to U+001F: `\b \t \n \f \r` where defined, `\u00xx` with
   125	  lowercase hex otherwise. Every other scalar appears literally.
   126	
   127	*Reason: R-3.1 binds to RFC 8785. Vectors C-04 and C-05 pin the escape and the UTF-16 ordering traps, for example
   128	U+1F600 sorting before U+E000.*
   129	
   130	### 1.4 Decoding, and the order in which rejections are reported
   131	
   132	Two entry points.
   133	- `ingest(text)` takes requester JSON: whitespace is allowed, and the depth limit is 62.
   134	- `payload(bytes)` takes in-frame bytes: the depth limit is 64, and the bytes must be canonical.
   135	
   136	Each runs these checks in this order and reports the **first category that fails**, scanning the whole value for
   137	each category. *Reason: both implementations then agree on the reason code, not only on rejection.*
   138	
   139	1. `JSON_ENCODING`: the bytes are not strict UTF-8. Overlongs, encoded surrogates and values above U+10FFFF are
   140	   rejected.
   141	2. `JSON_SYNTAX`:
   142	   - a BOM; not exactly one RFC 8259 JSON text; trailing non-whitespace;
   143	   - a raw control character in a string; bad escapes; leading zeros; `+`; `.5`; `1.`; `NaN`; `Infinity`.
   144	3. `JSON_DEPTH`.
   145	4. `JSON_DUPLICATE_NAME`.
   146	5. `JSON_STRING`: a surrogate, a noncharacter, an unassigned code point, or text that is not NFC.
   147	6. `JSON_NUMBER`: §1.2.
   148	7. *(`payload` only)* `JSON_NONCANONICAL`: the bytes are not equal to `JCS` of the parsed value.
   149	
   150	---
   151	
   152	## 2. The tagged byte strings: one header, four tags
   153	
   154	Every V1 byte string that a V1 key signs or that OTS stamps begins with the same header:
   155	
   156	```
   157	header(t) = u8 len(t) || t                       t ∈ T (ASCII, no NUL)
   158	```
   159	
   160	| Tag `t` | len | First bytes (hex) | Construction |
   161	|---|---|---|---|
   162	| `tessera-v1/attest` | 17 | `11 74 65 73 …` | frame, §3 |
   163	| `tessera-v1/manifest` | 19 | `13 74 65 73 …` | frame, §4 |
   164	| `tessera-v1/receipt-anchor` | 25 | `19 74 65 73 …` | anchor preimage, §5 |
   165	| `tessera-v1/dnssec-snapshot` | 26 | `1a 74 65 73 …` | snapshot preimage, §6 |
   166	
   167	- One string serves as both the domain-separation tag and the object type. R-3.11 lists the two as separate items,
   168	  but A1 P8's four-field frame has a single slot. The prefix `tessera-v1/` does the domain separation, and the
   169	  suffix names the type (G-07 resolved).
   170	- A tag outside T is rejected with `TAG_UNKNOWN`. That includes P7 types V1 does not use (`tessera-v1/wrapper`)
   171	  and later versions (`tessera-v2/attest`). A tag in T that is not the expected one is rejected with
   172	  `TAG_MISMATCH`. *Reason: these are the registered "type-tag mismatch" rejections, and the tag decides the
   173	  construction before any other byte is read.*
   174	- The length byte makes T prefix-free as headers. The proof's Lemma 4 depends on that.
   175	- *Why ASCII tags:* a reader with no V1 software can see them in a hex dump. That helps C6.
   176	
   177	---
   178	
   179	## 3. The attestation frame (one per signer)
   180	
   181	### 3.1 Layout (the four registered fields, A1 P8 / A7 §A7.1)
   182	
   183	```
   184	frame(t, p) = header(t) || u16 canonicalization_version || u32 payload_length || payload
   185	            with canonicalization_version = 0x0001 and payload_length = len(payload) ≤ 2^24
   186	```
   187	
   188	| Offset | Width | Field | Value / rule | Reason |
   189	|---|---|---|---|---|
   190	| 0 | 1 | tag length | `0x11` for attest | prefix-free tags |
   191	| 1 | 17 | `type_tag` | `tessera-v1/attest` | §2 |
   192	| 18 | 2 | `canonicalization_version` | `0x0001` means RFC 8785 + §1's domain + §1.2's number rule. Any other value: `CV_UNKNOWN` | fixed width; G-02 resolved (u16, value 1) |
   193	| 20 | 4 | `payload_length` | the exact payload length, ≤ 2^24, else `LENGTH_CAP` | G-01 changed from u64 to u32. Four bytes cover the cap, and a smaller field is one less thing to get wrong |
   194	| 24 | L | `payload` | `JCS(value)`, §3.2 | A7 §A7.1: the canonical payload bytes |
   195	
   196	The frame ends **exactly** at offset 24 + L.
   197	- If fewer than L payload bytes remain, the result is `TRUNCATED`. Truncation inside the header also gives
   198	  `TRUNCATED`.
   199	- If more remain, the result is `TRAILING`.
   200	
   201	The registered rejections "length-prefix mismatch" and "trailing bytes" are therefore one condition, reported by
   202	its direction. Once the length is the only payload boundary, a short length and a trailing byte are the same
   203	event.
   204	
   205	### 3.2 The attestation payload
   206	
   207	The payload is exactly this object. **No member may be missing or added** (`SCHEMA`). *Reason: a closed schema
   208	means no unsigned or unknown field can carry meaning, which is R-3.39.*
   209	
   210	```
   211	{"common":{
   212	   "alg":                 A,     ^[A-Z0-9][A-Z0-9-]{0,63}$          V1 issues "OPENPGP4-ECDSA-P256-SHA256"
   213	   "binding_forms":       [F…],  nonempty, strictly ascending, each ^[A-Z][A-Z0-9_]{0,31}$
   214	   "content":             <any value in V>                          the requester's content
   215	   "declared_issue_time": "YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ssZ"                    a real UTC date and time, seconds 00–59
   216	   "issuer":              D,     lowercase LDH name, ≥ 2 labels, labels 1–63, total ≤ 241
   217	   "manifest_sha256":     hex64  hex(SHA256(manifest frame)), §4
   218	   "preamble_sha256":     hex64  hex(SHA256(purpose-preamble bytes))
   219	   "profile":             "tessera-v1-strict"                       exact
   220	   "spec":{"id": S,  ^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9._/-]{0,127}$                  V1: "tessera-v1-strict/verification-spec/1"
   221	           "sha256": hex64}      hex(SHA256(specification bytes))
   222	   "vectors_sha256":      hex64  hex(SHA256(conformance-vector bytes))
   223	 },
   224	 "signer":{"key_fp": FP}}        FP ∈ §3.3
   225	```
   226	
   227	`hex64` means exactly 64 **lowercase** hex digits. Members appear in JCS order, which is the order shown.
   228	
   229	| Field | Reason, and the G-id it resolves |
   230	|---|---|
   231	| `alg`: any well-formed id, not only V1's | P8 accepts a well-formed unknown id, and stage (c) returns `UNVERIFIABLE`. The canary (R-3.53) needs this, and G-10b's failure mode is avoided. The id names the scheme: "a v4 OpenPGP binary-document signature whose document is the frame, ECDSA P-256, SHA-256" (G-57 kept). Its location inside the payload is A3 §A3.6.1's. G-11 is resolved for V1. |
   232	| `binding_forms`: a sorted, unique, nonempty array | R-3.37 says P8 "defines the set's canonical encoding, ordering, uniqueness and bounds". It sits in the attestation's signed bytes (G-17 kept). *Which* forms V1 requires is C4 profile work. The vectors use `["STMT_DIRECT"]` for illustration. |
   233	| `declared_issue_time`: second precision, `Z` | One spelling per instant. No leap second, because Unix time and Bitcoin time have none. G-16 kept. |
   234	| `issuer` = D, at most 241 characters | D is bound in the signed bytes for the owner-name check (C4, G-21). 241 keeps `_tessera-v1.<D>` within DNS's 255-octet name limit (§7). |
   235	| `manifest_sha256` over the manifest **frame** | The hash covers a tagged string, so it is domain-separated by construction. G-14 is resolved: the hash covers the frame of the tuple and nothing more. |
   236	| `spec`, `vectors_sha256`, `preamble_sha256` | R-3.50: the spec id and hash are in the signed bytes. The vectors and preamble are bound the same way. Each hash is over the object's raw bytes, so `sha256sum` reproduces it with no Tessera code. G-18 and G-47 are resolved as far as P8 goes: they are bound. Consuming them is verifier work. |
   237	| `profile` inside `common` | The signed bytes name their own profile (C6). Dispatch reads the bundle's unsigned profile label first (§8). |
   238	| `content` is arbitrary V | Any JSON value in V. A bare digest string is accepted (C-06 and F-03). |
   239	
   240	### 3.3 Fingerprint slot encodings (G-10 resolved; owner ruling for the DNS record)
   241	
   242	| Slot | `key_fp` string | Definition |
   243	|---|---|---|
   244	| `kms` | `spki-sha256:` + 64 hex | hex(SHA256(DER SubjectPublicKeyInfo)). For P-256 the SPKI is the 26-byte prefix `3059301306072a8648ce3d020106082a8648ce3d030107034200` followed by `04‖X‖Y` (91 bytes). This is what KMS `GetPublicKey` returns. |
   245	| `gpg` | `gpg-sha256:` + 64 hex | hex(SHA256(body)), where `body` is the v4 public-key packet body (RFC 9580 §5.5.2): `04` ‖ u32 creation ‖ `13` ‖ `08 2a8648ce3d030107` ‖ u16 `0x0203` ‖ `04‖X‖Y`. These are exactly the bytes that the v4 fingerprint hashes *after* its `0x99` ‖ u16 length prefix. That prefix is **not** included, and SHA-1 is not used. This is **not** the fingerprint gpg prints; a tool computes it from the exported key packet. |
   246	
   247	- The scheme prefix makes the two slots' strings differ in their first byte, so they can never be equal. It also
   248	  makes the frame self-describing.
   249	- The DNS record (§7) carries the bare hex under the slot name. The verifier compares `kms=<h>` with
   250	  `spki-sha256:<h>` and `gpg=<h>` with `gpg-sha256:<h>`.
   251	- *Consequences to record:*
   252	  - V1 uses no SHA-1 for key binding. The gpg slot binds the whole key packet body, creation time included, under
   253	    SHA-256.
   254	  - The KMS key's OpenPGP key-packet creation time is **not** covered by its `spki-sha256` fingerprint. This is
   255	    **accepted**: for the KMS slot the verifier binds the SPKI (the key KMS actually holds), not the OpenPGP key
   256	    packet. The OpenPGP wrapper's creation time therefore carries no meaning for that slot.
   257	
   258	### 3.4 The two frames, as bytes (A5 §A5.4 and R-3.12 stated over bytes)
   259	
   260	For one receipt with common value `C`:
   261	
   262	```
   263	F_kms = 11 'tessera-v1/attest' 0001 u32(L_kms) '{"common":' JCS(C) ',"signer":{"key_fp":' JCS(fp_kms) '}}'
   264	F_gpg = 11 'tessera-v1/attest' 0001 u32(L_gpg) '{"common":' JCS(C) ',"signer":{"key_fp":' JCS(fp_gpg) '}}'
   265	```
   266	
   267	- `JCS(C)` occupies bytes **[34, 34 + |JCS(C)|)** of both frames and is byte-identical. That covers the requester
   268	  content and every common binding.
   269	- The frames differ only in bytes 20–23 (the length) and in the `signer` member. In F-01/F-02 they first differ
   270	  at offset 23, then from offset 745, and the lengths differ by 1 byte (the prefixes `spki-sha256:` and
   271	  `gpg-sha256:`).
   272	- Payload-byte identity of `common` is therefore **not** frame equality. The two frames are never equal (C1).
   273	- No slot name is written: A5 §A5.4 says only the fingerprint differs. The slot comes from the manifest's order:
   274	  `key_fp` is slot `kms` and `sset[0]` is slot `gpg` (G-09 kept). A frame in the wrong slot gives
   275	  `RECEIPT_SLOT`.
   276	
   277	### 3.5 What exactly is signed (OpenPGP trailer, PHASE3 finding 4)
   278	
   279	Each slot's signature is an OpenPGP v4 signature of **type 0x00 (binary document)** whose document is the frame.
   280	The signed hash input is `frame ‖ trailer`, where:
   281	
   282	```
   283	trailer = 04 ‖ 00 ‖ pubalg ‖ hashalg ‖ u16 hlen ‖ hashed-subpackets ‖ 04 FF ‖ u32(6 + hlen)
   284	```
   285	
   286	- The trailer's last four bytes give its own length, so `frame ‖ trailer` splits into frame and trailer reading
   287	  from the end, and the frame's own `payload_length` then delimits the payload. PROOF.md Lemma 7 covers this.
   288	- **Signature type 0x01 (text) must be rejected:** text mode rewrites line endings, and frame headers and lengths
   289	  can contain the bytes `0x0a` and `0x0d`.
   290	- The unhashed subpacket area carries no meaning, and no V1 identifier is derived from signature bytes (W1).
   291	
   292	---
   293	
   294	## 4. The manifest frame (map-v1 tuple; G-08, G-14, G-22)
   295	
   296	```
   297	F_manifest = frame("tessera-v1/manifest", JCS({"alg":A,"issuer":D,"key_fp":fp_kms,"sset":[fp_gpg],"ver":"map-v1"}))
   298	manifest_sha256 = hex(SHA256(F_manifest))
   299	```
   300	
   301	- The schema is closed: `key_fp` must be a `spki-sha256:` string, and `sset` must hold exactly one `gpg-sha256:`
   302	  string. `ver` is exactly `map-v1`. Anything else is `SCHEMA`.
   303	- *Reason: this is the map-v1 tuple of R-3.36 in the slot order of the C-C2 trace (G-09). Each key's possession
   304	  proof (R-3.38) is its OpenPGP signature over this same frame. The `tessera-v1/manifest` tag keeps that
   305	  signature apart from the same key's attestation signature (P7).*
   306	
   307	## 5. The receipt-anchor preimage (G-19 and G-59 resolved; K-2)
   308	
   309	```
   310	A = header("tessera-v1/receipt-anchor") ‖ u8 0x02 ‖ u32 len(F_kms) ‖ F_kms ‖ u32 len(F_gpg) ‖ F_gpg
   311	anchor digest = SHA256(A), the value OpenTimestamps stamps
   312	```
   313	
   314	- Frames appear in **manifest slot order**.
   315	- The count byte must be 2 (`ANCHOR_COUNT`).
   316	- Each slot is decoded as a complete attestation frame, with its own length checked.
   317	- Bytes after the second frame give `TRAILING`.
   318	- *Reason:* one OTS proof must cover a receipt that has two frames (R-3.42 with reading (a)). Length prefixes and
   319	  whole frames make the preimage injective without any hash assumption. The tag keeps it from being taken for a
   320	  snapshot's proof.
   321	
   322	## 6. The DNSSEC-snapshot preimage
   323	
   324	```
   325	S = header("tessera-v1/dnssec-snapshot") ‖ u32 len(snapshot) ‖ snapshot        1 ≤ len ≤ 2^24
   326	```
   327	
   328	- The snapshot body's internal format belongs to the profile document (Track 3). P8 fixes only its envelope.
   329	- *Reason:* C4's snapshot rule gives the snapshot its own OTS proof. The tag keeps that proof from being
   330	  presented as a receipt anchor, or the reverse.
   331	
   332	---
   333	
   334	## 7. DNS key-publication record
   335	
   336	C4(i) needs a DNSSEC-authenticated record that binds V1's two keys to D. Neither the spec nor wamason.com has one
   337	yet (spike report; R-4.26 says only "DNSSEC records publishing the fingerprints").
   338	
   339	**Owner name:** `_tessera-v1.<D>`, where D is `common.issuer`.
   340	- The verifier rebuilds this name from the signed `issuer` and compares it, label by label and ignoring ASCII
   341	  case, with the validated RRset's owner name. Anything else gives `DNS_OWNER`.
   342	- *Reason:* the owner name itself is then the C4 owner-name check (G-21/M2). An underscore label cannot collide
   343	  with a hostname, and the `-v1` suffix lets a later profile publish next to it.
   344	
   345	**Type:** TXT. **RRset:** exactly **one** TXT record at the owner name (`DNS_RRSET` if there are zero or more).
   346	**RDATA:** exactly **one** character-string (`DNS_STRINGS` otherwise). If a string's length byte runs past the
   347	RDATA, the result is `DNS_RDATA`.
   348	- *Reason:* the longest canonical value is 196 bytes, under the 255-byte limit for one string. That removes every
   349	  question about how several strings concatenate. A provider that splits a short value is non-conforming for V1.
   350	
   351	**Value grammar** (ABNF; the canonical form is the only accepted form):
   352	
   353	```
   354	value      = "v=tessera1" ";profile=tessera-v1-strict" ";kms=" 64LHEX ";gpg=" 64LHEX [ ";not-before=" date ]
   355	LHEX       = %x30-39 / %x61-66                              ; lowercase hex only
   356	date       = 4DIGIT "-" 2DIGIT "-" 2DIGIT                   ; a real Gregorian date
   357	```
   358	
   359	- `kms` is the hex of §3.3's `spki-sha256`. `gpg` is the hex of §3.3's `gpg-sha256`.
   360	- `not-before` is optional. What it means for a declared time is C4 profile text, not P8.
   361	- The value has no spaces and no trailing `;`. Field order is fixed. Bytes are printable ASCII from 0x21 to 0x7E.
   362	
   363	Example (DNS-02):
   364	
   365	```
   366	v=tessera1;profile=tessera-v1-strict;kms=5cd252fb…7cd3;gpg=5ecd7121…8fc3;not-before=2026-10-12
   367	```
   368	
   369	**Rejection order** (the first that applies):
   370	1. `DNS_OWNER`
   371	2. `DNS_RRSET`
   372	3. `DNS_RDATA`
   373	4. `DNS_STRINGS`
   374	5. `DNS_SYNTAX`:
   375	   - a byte outside 0x21–0x7E;
   376	   - a field that is not `key=value`, with key `[a-z][a-z-]*` and a nonempty value containing no `=`;
   377	   - an empty field.
   378	6. `DNS_VERSION`: the first field is not `v=tessera1`, or `v` appears again.
   379	7. `DNS_FIELD_UNKNOWN`: an extra field.
   380	8. `DNS_SLOT_DUPLICATE` (`kms` or `gpg` appears twice) or `DNS_FIELD_DUPLICATE` (another field appears twice).
   381	9. `DNS_SLOT_MISSING`.
   382	10. `DNS_FIELD_MISSING` (`profile`).
   383	11. `DNS_ORDER`.
   384	12. `DNS_PROFILE`.
   385	13. `DNS_VALUE`: the hex or the date fails its grammar.
   386	
   387	**Why the record is unambiguous** (outside PROOF.md's frame domain):
   388	- The accepted values form a regular language. Every field begins with a fixed literal key, and every value
   389	  comes from an alphabet without `;` or `=`, so splitting on `;` and then on the first `=` recovers the fields
   390	  uniquely.
   391	- With the field order fixed and every value having exactly one spelling (lowercase hex, `YYYY-MM-DD`), the map
   392	  from (kms, gpg, not-before) to value strings is injective, and its inverse is the parse.
   393	- One RR with one character-string removes concatenation ambiguity. The RDATA length byte delimits the string.
   394	- The owner name is compared as a label sequence, so `_tessera-v1.wamason.com` cannot match
   395	  `_tessera-v1.wamason.com.evil.example` or `tessera-v1.wamason.com` (XD-12 to XD-14).
   396	- The value begins `v=` (0x76), while every tagged string in §2 begins with a byte from 0x11 to 0x1a. So a TXT
   397	  value can never be a V1 frame, and a frame can never be a TXT value.
   398	
   399	The git channel's key-assertion blob is not specified here. *Suggestion:* publish this same canonical value as the
   400	blob's content, so both channels assert one byte string.
   401	
   402	---
   403	
   404	## 8. Identifiers and versions, collected
   405	
   406	| Identifier | Encoding | Where |
   407	|---|---|---|
   408	| Construction / object type | `tessera-v1/{attest,manifest,receipt-anchor,dnssec-snapshot}` | §2 header |
   409	| Canonicalization version | u16 `0x0001` | frame offset 18 |
   410	| Profile | `"tessera-v1-strict"` | `common.profile`; DNS `profile=` |
   411	| Verification spec | `common.spec.id` + `common.spec.sha256` | §3.2 |
   412	| Authority statement version | `"map-v1"` | manifest `ver` |
   413	| DNS record version | `v=tessera1` | §7 |
   414	| Algorithm | `common.alg` | §3.2 |
   415	
   416	**Dispatch boundary (O-78, an owner decision):**
   417	- The verifier dispatches on the bundle's **unsigned** profile label. An unrecognized label gives
   418	  `UNVERIFIABLE / OUT_OF_PROFILE`, and nothing in this document is evaluated.
   419	- Once a bundle is dispatched to `tessera-v1-strict`, every rejection here is `INVALID`. That includes a signed
   420	  `profile` other than `tessera-v1-strict`, an unknown tag and an unknown canonicalization version, because the
   421	  bundle claimed V1 and its signed bytes contradict that claim.
   422	- The single in-profile `UNVERIFIABLE` that concerns these bytes is a well-formed unknown `alg` (stage (c)), and P8
   423	  lets that through.
   424	
   425	## 9. Owner decisions taken here, for the author's record
   426	
   427	D-1 **Number rule: value-preserving (§1.2).** This changes A1 P8's item "numbers not exactly representable as
   428	IEEE 754 doubles" and prereg §4.3's "must be exactly representable". **It needs an A9 sentence.** Codex review 14
   429	asked for exactly that. The registered wording also fails to compose with RFC 8785 (§1.2). The derivation's
   430	profile line, which says the rule is unchanged, must be revised to match.
   431	D-2 Unicode 16.0.0 is pinned. Unassigned code points are rejected, and text that is not NFC is rejected rather than
   432	normalized.
   433	D-3 Limits: a payload of at most 2^24 bytes; depth 64 for the payload and 62 for content.
   434	D-4 Widths: a u8 tag length, a u16 canonicalization version equal to 1, and a u32 length (G-01 changed from u64).
   435	D-5 One tag for both signers' frames (A5 §A5.4). Slots are told apart by the fingerprint and the manifest order,
   436	and no slot name is written.
   437	D-6 Fingerprint strings carry scheme prefixes: `spki-sha256` for kms and `gpg-sha256` for gpg.
   438	**Owner decision (2026-10-09): no SHA-1 in V1.** The gpg key is bound by SHA-256 over its OpenPGP v4 public-key
   439	packet body, not by the v4 SHA-1 fingerprint, and this applies to the frame, the manifest and the DNS record. The
   440	KMS key stays SHA-256 of its SPKI. Its OpenPGP creation time is unbound, and that is accepted because the
   441	verifier binds the SPKI for that slot (§3.3).
   442	D-7 `manifest_sha256` is computed over the manifest frame. Spec, vectors and preamble are bound by SHA-256 of their
   443	raw bytes.
   444	D-8 The anchor preimage holds the full frames in manifest slot order, with a count byte. The DNSSEC snapshot has its
   445	own tagged envelope.
   446	D-9 A closed schema. `declared_issue_time` has second precision with no leap second. D is at most 241 characters.
   447	`binding_forms` is a sorted array.
   448	D-10 OpenPGP signature type 0x00 only.
   449	D-11 The dispatch boundary in §8.
   450	D-12 The DNS record: one RR, one string, a fixed order, no spaces, and the owner name compared without regard to
   451	case.
   452	D-13 Reason codes, and the order in which they are reported (§1.4, §3.1, §7). "Trailing bytes" and "length-prefix
   453	mismatch" are reported as `TRAILING` or `TRUNCATED`.
   454	
   455	**Out of P8's scope, and named so that nothing is dropped quietly:**
   456	- the bundle's container format and transport encoding (G-26);
   457	- where the observed anchor delay is recorded (G-40);
   458	- the snapshot body's format;
   459	- the git blob path;
   460	- the signature verification profile (P3 [assumption]);
   461	- which binding forms V1 requires.
