    37	## 1. The claim
    38	
    39	**V1 claim (one sentence).** *Conditional on the registered cryptographic and key-use assumptions, a V1 receipt
    40	lets anyone holding it establish, without Tessera, that the attested content was presented to the Tessera V1
    41	service, whose own domain is D; that each of the service's two keys K = {K_kms, K_gpg} signed its own frame
    42	binding that content; that two named channels authenticated these keys for D at the stated evidence times; and
    43	that the signed frames existed no later than T. Effective revocation is not evaluated, and the receipt attests
    44	neither the submitter nor the content's meaning. The verdict is typed and says what was not established.*
    45	
    46	V1 is, plainly, a dual-signed timestamp notary for Tessera's own domain. Its comparator is an RFC 3161 TSA token
    47	plus an OTS proof. Its two differentiators are verification with no network and no live party, and authority
    48	evidenced through DNSSEC and a time-anchored repository assertion instead of a CA.
    49	
    50	| # | Atomic sub-claim | Non-claim kept beside it |
    51	|---|---|---|
    52	| C1 | **Bytes.** The verifier reconstructs each required signer's exact frame from the bundle. Both frames bind the same attested content and common metadata, with the designated signer's fingerprint in its slot. The receipt-anchor preimage over the two frames is defined and unambiguous. | Nothing about meaning or truth (A3 §A3.1). Payload-byte identity is not equality of the two frames. |
    53	| C2 | **Presentation.** The content was presented at some issuance event of the V1 service (existential, not unique). | Not who submitted it (f), nor whether a replay suits the transaction (R-1.5). |
    54	| C3 | **Signature.** Each required signature verifies over its designated frame; a missing, stripped or substituted one fails. The symbolic queries reject reattribution of the modelled signed bytes through the required key/frame bindings. | DSKS was only emulated in the prototype; concrete library/curve resistance is H1a evidence (P3 assumption half). The two keys are separate custody and failure domains, not independent approval of content. Nothing about the key lifecycle after T. |
    55	| C4 | **Authority.** The named channels authenticated K for D at their stated evidence times: (i) a DNSSEC chain whose owner name is D, captured at issuance, validated against the verifier's own root-key configuration at the snapshot's own anchored time; (ii) an authenticated, time-anchored key assertion under the distributed repository key (a signed, OTS-anchored commit whose full commit→tree→blob path is verified). The verifier fixes the required set. | **Effective revocation is not evaluated.** Not public publication of the commit. Not the domain holder's honesty; whole-author-sphere capture is R-2.15. |
    56	| C5 | **Time.** The framed bytes existed no later than T, the earliest verified attestation **in the presented OTS proof**. The authenticated anchor and k-th confirmation header timestamps satisfy the registered three-conjunct predicate. This is presentation-sensitive: a different presentation of the same receipt can select a different T. | No lower bound, no priority (A7 §A7.4). Header timestamps are chain-time proxies (skew, non-monotonic; A2.1). k = 6 assumes forging six mainnet blocks costs more than a receipt is worth, and canonical history at the pinned headers. A reorganization deeper than k is a residual. |
    57	| C6 | **Verifiable without Tessera.** The verdict is a pure function of the bundle, the published V1 verifier and its distributed trust configuration. No network, no author help. The bundle carries the verification-spec identifier and hash in the signed bytes, and the spec and conformance vectors as content-addressed objects. | Custody is the holder's job. Dead-project reconstruction is not demonstrated (§3, omission 15). A receipt anchored beyond the newest pinned header is `UNVERIFIABLE` until a later configuration is obtained (an availability limit). |
    58	| C7 | **Safe decision** (the 4 Dec acceptance criterion, not yet established). The verdict is typed. It reports `revocation: NOT_EVALUATED` and the standing value separately and never folds them in. Using the documentation alone, a non-expert makes no unsafe accept on the registered scenario mapping (A9.1). | Revocation (A9.4) and standing (A9.3) are not claimed. False rejects are allowed only on the registered cases. |
    59	
    60	**V1's fixed profile,** `tessera-v1-strict`, carried inside the signed bytes:
    61	- **Encoding.** `formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md`: RFC 8785 under the **value-preserving number rule** (accept a number exactly when RFC 8785's canonical form preserves its value; this replaces the registered literal rule, see A9.8), in tag-headed frames with u16 version and u32 length. The two signer frames share the common bytes and differ only in the fingerprint slot. KMS key: `sha256` of the SPKI. GPG key: `sha256` of the OpenPGP key packet (no SHA-1). DNS key publication: TXT at `_tessera-v1.<D>`. P8 prose proof: `formal/p8/PROOF.md`; vectors with two agreeing decoders in `formal/p8/vectors/`.
    62	- **Bundle contents.** Spec identifier and hash (signed); spec and conformance vectors (embedded, content-addressed); a bound plain-language purpose preamble; a dependency manifest (exact editions of RFC 8785, OpenPGP, ECDSA P-256, SHA-256, SHA-1 for git object linkage, the DNSSEC algorithms in the chain, OTS, Bitcoin headers).
    63	- **Signers.** KMS-backed OpenPGP P-256 plus gpg OpenPGP P-256 (P4'). GPG runs on author-controlled hardware outside AWS (A9.6).
    64	- **Authority.** DNSSEC for D plus the git key assertion, both required, n = 2.
    65	- **Trust configuration** (ships with the verifier, never taken from the bundle): root KSK-2017 and KSK-2024, each with its IANA validity period (KSK-2024 signs the root from 2026-10-11; KSK-2017 is revoked 2027-01-11 and removed 2027-03-22); the repository key; a signed, versioned set of pinned mainnet header hashes covering accepted histories.
    66	- **Verification.** Strict only. One layer, no wrappers, no renewal, no TLR, no waiver policy. Revocation not evaluated.
    67	- **Parameters.** δ = 72 h, ε = 24 h, k = 6, N = 3, S = 24 h (A2's operational slack, wall clock).
    68	- **Issuance.** Operated API (API Gateway + Lambda + KMS). A receipt ships only when complete; refusal never concerns a shipped receipt.
    69	- **Dispatch.** An unrecognized profile, layer structure or request gets `UNVERIFIABLE / OUT_OF_PROFILE` without evaluating it. Inside the profile, A4.2's precedence holds: a failed required check gives `INVALID` even when another is unperformable.
