   334	## 7. DNS key-publication record
   335	
   336	C4(i) needs a DNSSEC-authenticated record that binds V1's two keys to D. Neither the spec nor wamason.com has one
   337	yet (spike report; R-4.26 says only "DNSSEC records publishing the fingerprints").
   338	
   339	**Owner name:** `_tessera-v1.<D>`, where D is `common.issuer`.
   340	- The verifier rebuilds this name from the signed `issuer` and compares it, label by label and ignoring ASCII
   341	  case, with the validated RRset's owner name. Anything else gives `DNS_OWNER`.
   342	- *Reason:* the owner name itself is then the C4 owner-name check (G-21/M2). An underscore label cannot collide
   343	  with a hostname, and the `-v1` suffix lets a later profile publish next to it.
   344	
   345	**Type:** TXT. **RRset:** exactly **one** TXT record at the owner name (`DNS_RRSET` if there are zero or more).
   346	**RDATA:** exactly **one** character-string (`DNS_STRINGS` otherwise). If a string's length byte runs past the
   347	RDATA, the result is `DNS_RDATA`.
   348	- *Reason:* the longest canonical value is 196 bytes, under the 255-byte limit for one string. That removes every
   349	  question about how several strings concatenate. A provider that splits a short value is non-conforming for V1.
   350	
   351	**Value grammar** (ABNF; the canonical form is the only accepted form):
   352	
   353	```
   354	value      = "v=tessera1" ";profile=tessera-v1-strict" ";kms=" 64LHEX ";gpg=" 64LHEX [ ";not-before=" date ]
   355	LHEX       = %x30-39 / %x61-66                              ; lowercase hex only
   356	date       = 4DIGIT "-" 2DIGIT "-" 2DIGIT                   ; a real Gregorian date
   357	```
   358	
   359	- `kms` is the hex of §3.3's `spki-sha256`. `gpg` is the hex of §3.3's `gpg-sha256`.
   360	- `not-before` is optional. What it means for a declared time is C4 profile text, not P8.
   361	- The value has no spaces and no trailing `;`. Field order is fixed. Bytes are printable ASCII from 0x21 to 0x7E.
   362	
   363	Example (DNS-02):
   364	
   365	```
   366	v=tessera1;profile=tessera-v1-strict;kms=5cd252fb…7cd3;gpg=5ecd7121…8fc3;not-before=2026-10-12
   367	```
   368	
   369	**Rejection order** (the first that applies):
   370	1. `DNS_OWNER`
   371	2. `DNS_RRSET`
   372	3. `DNS_RDATA`
   373	4. `DNS_STRINGS`
   374	5. `DNS_SYNTAX`:
   375	   - a byte outside 0x21–0x7E;
   376	   - a field that is not `key=value`, with key `[a-z][a-z-]*` and a nonempty value containing no `=`;
   377	   - an empty field.
   378	6. `DNS_VERSION`: the first field is not `v=tessera1`, or `v` appears again.
   379	7. `DNS_FIELD_UNKNOWN`: an extra field.
   380	8. `DNS_SLOT_DUPLICATE` (`kms` or `gpg` appears twice) or `DNS_FIELD_DUPLICATE` (another field appears twice).
   381	9. `DNS_SLOT_MISSING`.
   382	10. `DNS_FIELD_MISSING` (`profile`).
   383	11. `DNS_ORDER`.
   384	12. `DNS_PROFILE`.
   385	13. `DNS_VALUE`: the hex or the date fails its grammar.
   386	
   387	**Why the record is unambiguous** (outside PROOF.md's frame domain):
   388	- The accepted values form a regular language. Every field begins with a fixed literal key, and every value
   389	  comes from an alphabet without `;` or `=`, so splitting on `;` and then on the first `=` recovers the fields
   390	  uniquely.
   391	- With the field order fixed and every value having exactly one spelling (lowercase hex, `YYYY-MM-DD`), the map
   392	  from (kms, gpg, not-before) to value strings is injective, and its inverse is the parse.
   393	- One RR with one character-string removes concatenation ambiguity. The RDATA length byte delimits the string.
   394	- The owner name is compared as a label sequence, so `_tessera-v1.wamason.com` cannot match
   395	  `_tessera-v1.wamason.com.evil.example` or `tessera-v1.wamason.com` (XD-12 to XD-14).
   396	- The value begins `v=` (0x76), while every tagged string in §2 begins with a byte from 0x11 to 0x1a. So a TXT
   397	  value can never be a V1 frame, and a frame can never be a TXT value.
   398	
   399	The git channel's key-assertion blob is not specified here. *Suggestion:* publish this same canonical value as the
   400	blob's content, so both channels assert one byte string.
